\section{Qualifiers State What a Use May Assume}

A qualifier adds information to a type without necessarily changing its representation. A nonnull reference can be passed where a nullable one is expected, while the reverse direction needs evidence. A readonly view permits fewer operations than a writable one; it does not generally say the underlying object is immutable. Keep the declaration, which describes what assignments are allowed, separate from the current fact, which describes what is known at a particular program point. Under that distinction, \emph{flow sensitivity} is the ability to type the same source variable more precisely on one path than on another.

J.\ B.\ Wells, Allyn Dimock, Robert Muller, and Franklyn Turbak's \emph{A Typed Intermediate Language for Flow-Directed Compilation} represents flows explicitly in a typed intermediate language, using function labels and intersection and union types. The qualifier analysis below has a different domain, but shares their principle: if a transformation relies on a flow fact, the IR should say which value is refined and where the refinement is valid. A nominal type alone cannot express that provenance.

\section{Worked Flow: The Test Refines a Snapshot}

Consider a nullable field \texttt{next} and a function that may change it through an alias. In an SSA-like notation:
\begin{verbatim}
p0 = load(node.next)
if p0 != null:
    touch(other)
    use_nonnull(p0)
    p1 = load(node.next)
    use_nonnull(p1)   // requires separate evidence
\end{verbatim}
The test splits flow on the same value $p_0$. Assuming an ordinary managed reference and a call that cannot rewrite the SSA temporary itself, $p_0$ stays nonnull on the true edge even if the call stores null into \texttt{node.next}. The reload $p_1$ is not the tested value. Rewriting the first use to load the field again would be incorrect unless alias and effect analysis proves the intervening call cannot affect it. This reasoning does not turn a nonnull unmanaged pointer into a live or correctly aligned one; those are different facts.

Use a forward analysis with abstract values drawn from the powerset of $\{\mathsf{null},\mathsf{nonnull}\}$. The branch transfer intersects the incoming fact with the singleton appropriate to each edge. At a reachable merge, take the union of each incoming fact (for a phi parameter, translate the operands across their respective edges first). The empty set denotes an infeasible case; an unknown but reachable value is represented by the full set. At loops, iterate to a fixed point and recheck that every use that requires a precise fact is justified on every path. A separate abstract store can track fields keyed by a memory version or an abstract location; apply strong updates only when the location denotes a uniquely determined cell, otherwise conservatively merge or drop the fact.

