\section{Invalidation, Layout, and Verification}

Certify the checked value, not merely a location with the same spelling. The check elimination invariant is that each erased check has a dominating proof for its exact operand, or that the required fact is established on every predecessor of its defining phi. Facts about memory also require an appropriate no-clobber proof. Unknown calls, callbacks, and volatile reads need conservative transfer rules. Under concurrency even apparently identical reloads may differ; a verifier must use the guarantees of the memory model rather than assume that no visible assignment implies no change. Markdown Dossier Chapter~17 explains how explicit IR memory can expose these dependencies to a pass.

Satish Chandra and Thomas Reps's \emph{Physical Type Checking for C} illustrates a distinct low-level obligation: when typed field accesses survive pointer casts, safety depends on correspondence between the layouts of the actual and advertised objects at the offsets they use. A flow proof that excludes null cannot justify an access at an offset absent in the actual object, and identical offsets on one target do not show that a conversion is portable or valid for arbitrary C accesses. Track semantic properties and layout obligations separately in a typed IR. This also sharpens testing: a branch that removes null, a call that may clobber a field, and a nonnull pointer with insufficient layout evidence must produce three different checker outcomes.
