\section{Merging Phi Operands With Edge-Local Evidence}

The compiler can prove that a merged value is nonnull even when
neither of its possible operands is globally nonnull. The key is
to ask about each operand on the edge where the phi selects it.
Assume a forward SSA analysis has already computed sound edge
facts at a fixed point. Each edge is marked reachable or
unreachable, and each reachable edge maps value identities to
nullness sets. An absent entry means both null and nonnull are
possible. Given a phi with one operand per incoming edge, produce
the output qualifier and, when useful, the per-edge witnesses for
an independent verifier. No inference about the underlying heap
is needed for this calculation.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input phi arguments $A$, incoming edge facts $F$
  \Output merged fact and per-edge evidence
  \State $N\gets\varnothing$; $W\gets$ empty map
  \ForAll{$(e,v)$ in $A$}
    \If{$F[e]$ is unreachable}
      \State skip this edge \Comment{Not an unknown value}
    \Else
      \State $S\gets\Call{FactOrUnknown}{F[e],v}$
      \Comment{Look up the exact phi operand}
      \State $N\gets N\cup S$
      \State $W[e]\gets(v,S)$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $(N,W)$
\end{sudocode}

Let incoming edge $e_1$ come from the true arm of a test
\texttt{left != null}, and $e_2$ from the true arm of
\texttt{right != null}. In the merge, $p$ selects \texttt{left}
on $e_1$ and \texttt{right} on $e_2$. Even when \texttt{right}
is unknown on $e_1$ and \texttt{left} unknown on $e_2$, the
two lookups return $\{\mathsf{nonnull}\}$ and the union does
too. A check of $p$ can be removed under the stated assumptions.
Add a reachable $e_3$ passing a literal null: the result now
contains both possibilities, and the check remains. If $e_3$
is instead unreachable, it contributes nothing.

For any execution that reaches the merge, the concrete phi value
is contained in the operand set for its actual predecessor, and
therefore in the union. If no edge is reachable, the empty result
means that no runtime value was observed, not that the phi is
known nonnull. Track edge identities, not just predecessor block
labels, in IRs with multiple edges from one block. This query
is linear in the number of incoming edges with constant-time
fact lookups. Recompute the witnesses after edge rewrites; they
certify the current operand mapping only.
